title ALES68 — Lotus's multi-tap delay, several fixed taps of the dry signal
prompt recur the itinerary Lindy-first crux-first, take the next agent-doable lap, send each finished increment, stop at custody gates
think ALES66 opened the time-based wing with the FEEDBACK delay, where the delay line IS the clip buffer and each echo feeds the next. Its own comment names the road above it: chorus, flanger, reverb, each a delay line read a different way.
think Crux-first, the next most tractable way to read a delay line is the one a feedback loop cannot give: several FIXED taps of the DRY signal, each at its own delay and level, summed with the dry — the discrete early-reflection pattern a single feeding echo cannot voice. It is the building block the reverb's early-reflection network stands on, so Lindy-durable.
think The crux is the contrast with ALES66: the taps must read the ORIGINAL dry, never a written output, so they never feed each other. The exact idiom the tree already proves is the graft/clipboard snapshot-before-write (ALES24/25) — copy the dry prefix [0,end) into one bounded [max_clip]i16 scratch first, then every tap reads the frozen dry.
think Unlike the feedback echo where unity is a runaway refused BadFeedback, a multi-tap has no loop, so a tap at unity is legal (a full-level discrete echo); only num>den (a tap louder than the dry) and den==0 refuse BadLevel. The sum accumulates in i64 and saturates once (ALES3's one true saturation), so a constructive pile-up pins rather than wraps.
obs GREEN on the first build across the six laws — no RED this round. The sharpest proof of independence: a two-tap impulse's tap-4 copy is 1/4 of the ORIGINAL 8000 (= 2000), NOT the compounded feedback value (1000) an ALES66 train would land — the taps read the frozen dry, neither feeds the other.
obs Order-independence proven directly: the same three-tap set forward and reversed lands byte-for-byte identical audio, because every tap reads the frozen snapshot and no tap sees another's write.
obs Scratch cost is one [4096]i16 (8 KB) on the stack — the exact width and stack cost graft already carries with two such buffers, so no new bound introduced.
loom witness=ales_taps_witness cases=6 green=1 reruns=ales3_mix+ales66_echo max_clip=4096 max_taps=16 scratch_bytes=8192
